\f1\b\fs22 The Battle for Sublime Point: A Morning of Magnificent Mayhem
\f2\b0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dawn broke over Sublime Point with all the subtlety of a particularly aggressive alarm clock, revealing a tableau that would have impressed the most ambitious war correspondent and deeply confused any passing ornithologist. The government forces had spent the night arranging themselves in what Gerry optimistically called "optimal assault formation," which looked remarkably like a very large, very well-armed picnic that had gotten seriously out of hand.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>At precisely 0600 hours, Dunhilton's forces launched their attack with the kind of technological sophistication that suggested someone had been reading too many military procurement brochures. Plasma cannons hummed to life, electromagnetic pulse generators began their ominous charging cycles, and various pieces of equipment that nobody fully understood but which made impressive whooshing sounds were pointed menacingly at Sublime Point's walls.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>For approximately seventeen minutes, it was the most spectacular light show anyone had witnessed outside of a particularly ambitious fireworks display. Eloise's defensive systems responded with equal enthusiasm, deploying countermeasures that had been designed by engineers who clearly believed that the best defense was a really, really good offense that happened to be pointing in the opposite direction.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Energy shields clashed with particle beams, creating patterns in the air that resembled either advanced physics or an extremely expensive screensaver. Quantum disruptors met reality stabilizers in confrontations that caused local birds to question whether they were still operating under the same laws of physics they had been using yesterday.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The result, after twenty-three minutes of sound and fury that accomplished absolutely nothing except providing excellent footage for Alexander's continued broadcast, was a technological stalemate so complete that both sides' equipment appeared to have agreed to take a coffee break.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Hand-to-Hand Solution
\f2\b0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Sir," Gerry's communications officer reported with the tone of someone delivering news that was both expected and deeply unwelcome, "all our advanced systems have been neutralized. Their defensive grid is matching our offensive capabilities almost perfectly."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry studied the tactical displays showing two forces of roughly equal technological sophistication locked in what amounted to an extremely expensive standoff. "Recommendations?"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Ground assault, sir. Hand-to-hand combat with conventional weapons. Their tech can't neutralize what isn't there to neutralize."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>It was, Gerry reflected, rather like discovering that the solution to advanced calculus was simply to count on your fingers, but louder and with significantly more potential for grievous bodily harm.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The order went out: fix bayonets, check ammunition, and prepare for the kind of warfare that had been practiced since humans first discovered that hitting people with sticks was both effective and emotionally satisfying.\

\f1\b <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Elvi Cavalry
\f2\b0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The High Elvis had been watching the technological light show from his command tank with the expression of someone observing a particularly elaborate mating dance between two species of exotic electronics. When the government forces began their ground advance, he reached for his sequined megaphone with the satisfaction of a conductor finally getting to start the symphony.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"All units," he announced across the Elvi communication network, "the time has come to demonstrate the superiority of Elvis-based warfare over conventional military tactics. Remember\'97we fight not just for ourselves, but for the Holy Momma, the Great Teacher, and the principle that rock and roll will never die, even if everything else does."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Elvi charge began with four hundred voices raised in a battle hymn that was either "Love Me Tender" or a war cry so primal that it transcended conventional musical categories. They poured down from their positions like a sequined avalanche, brandishing weapons that ranged from military-grade assault rifles to baseball bats decorated with rhinestones and what appeared to be a full drum kit being wielded as a siege engine.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The superior numbers of the Elvi forces immediately began to balance out the government's advantage in conventional military training, creating a battlefield that resembled either a very large bar fight or a musical performance that had gotten seriously out of hand.\

\f1\b <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Gyges Disc Gambit
\f2\b0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>It was Specialist Tulsa McLean who first suggested using their most unusual tactical advantage. "High Elvis," she called over the sound of combat, "the guitar cases!"\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Elvi had arrived carrying their instrument cases with the devotion that musicians typically reserved for their favorite guitars. What the government forces had not anticipated was that these cases contained not musical instruments, but carefully cushioned Gyges Discs\'97dozens of them, salvaged from the mountain mansion heist and transported with the kind of care usually reserved for unstable explosives.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Which, in a sense, they were.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>"Remember," the High Elvis announced as his forces began opening their cases, "these things are more dangerous than anything the government has. Handle with extreme care, throw with extreme prejudice, and if anyone sees blue lightning, everybody runs in the opposite direction."\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The sight of Elvi warriors hurling gleaming metal discs through the air like the world's most dangerous frisbee tournament created a new dynamic on the battlefield. The Gyges Discs, when thrown with sufficient force and landed with sufficient impact, began generating localized quantum disturbances that manifested as small earthquakes, reality fluctuations, and in one memorable case, a brief reversal of local gravity that left three government soldiers hanging upside down in mid-air for seventeen seconds.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The effect was to transform the battle from a conventional military engagement into something that resembled a cross between a medieval siege and a physics experiment that had achieved consciousness and decided to take sides.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Government forces found themselves dodging not just conventional weapons, but flying discs that could cause the ground beneath their feet to question its fundamental commitment to remaining solid. Elvi warriors discovered that their cache of reality-warping artifacts was surprisingly effective at equalizing the technological disparity, assuming one didn't mind the occasional localized apocalypse as a side effect.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Battle for Sublime Point had become exactly the kind of conflict that would perplex military historians for generations\'97a fight where superior firepower was countered by superior numbers, which were in turn countered by weapons that occasionally redefined the basic laws of physics on a case-by-case basis.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>In his mobile command unit, Gerry watched the tactical displays with the expression of someone trying to solve a chess problem where half the pieces had suddenly decided to play by checkers rules and the other half were apparently making up their own rules entirely.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The morning was progressing exactly as chaotically as anyone might have predicted, and it was only just getting started.
